---
import { Kbd, KbdGroup } from "@dicehub/kappa/components/kbd";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import CopyPageControls from "../../../components/CopyPageControls.vue";
import DocsPageNavigation from "../../../components/DocsPageNavigation.astro";
import SidebarBlocksDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarBlocksDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarGroupedDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarGroupedDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarSubmenusDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarSubmenusDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarDropdownSubmenusDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarDropdownSubmenusDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarInboxDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarInboxDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarWorkspacePagesDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarWorkspacePagesDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarMinimalWorkspaceDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarMinimalWorkspaceDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarPaneledWorkspaceDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarPaneledWorkspaceDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarHoverRevealDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarHoverRevealDocsDemo.vue";
import SidebarLayoutStateDocsDemo from "../../../components/SidebarLayoutStateDocsDemo.vue";
import controlledSource from "../../../components/SidebarLayoutStateDocsDemo.vue?raw";
import { sidebarBlocks } from "../../../data/sidebar-blocks";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/docs-components.css";
import "../../../styles/docs-blocks.css";

const currentPath = "/docs/blocks/appshell";
const description = "Application shell layouts built from Kappa controls, with responsive navigation and source you can copy.";
const starterSources = import.meta.glob("../../../snippets/app-shell/*.vue", { eager: true, query: "?raw", import: "default" }) as Record<string, string>;
const exampleSource = (variant: string) => starterSources[`../../../snippets/app-shell/${variant}.vue`];
---

<DocsLayout title="Application Shell" description={description}>
  <div class="docs-page docs-page--component docs-page--blocks">
    <header class="docs-page-header"><div class="docs-page-header__inner"><div class="docs-page-header__content">
      <div class="docs-page-header__mobile-actions"><CopyPageControls client:only="vue" align="center" /></div>
      <div class="docs-page-header__title-row"><h1>Application Shell</h1><div class="docs-page-header__navigation-actions">
        <div class="docs-page-header__desktop-actions"><CopyPageControls client:only="vue" /></div>
        <DocsPageNavigation currentPath={currentPath} variant="compact" />
      </div></div>
      <p class="docs-page-header__lede">{description}</p>
    </div></div></header>
    <div class="docs-page-body">
      <article class="docs-article docs-article--component">
        <section id="overview" class="docs-section">
          <h2><a class="docs-section-anchor" href="#overview">Overview</a></h2>
          <p>Choose a layout, then copy its shell starter. AppShell handles the layout, navigation surface, and panels. Namespace, profile, search, navigation, and New project controls work with local example data. No account, router, or backend is connected.</p>
          <p><a href="/docs/components/sidebar">Sidebar component</a> documents the primitive parts, resizing, peeking, loading, and sliding views. The examples below show these parts in complete application layouts.</p>
          <div class="docs-api-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Kappa component</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/sidebar">Sidebar</a></td><td>Navigation, resizing, collapsible sections, and the mobile drawer.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/dropdown">Dropdown</a></td><td>Namespace, profile, and page-link menus.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/command-palette">Command Palette</a></td><td>Quick search and keyboard navigation between pages.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/button">Button</a></td><td>Page actions such as New project.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/table">Table</a></td><td>Compact project lists in the examples.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/tabs">Tabs</a> and <a href="/docs/components/toggle-group">Toggle Group</a></td><td>Page views, category tabs, and simulation run status filters.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/activity-feed">Activity Feed</a></td><td>Project updates grouped by date.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/hover-card">Hover Card</a></td><td>Category flyouts with keyboard and pointer support.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/dialog">Dialog</a></td><td>Search dialogs and contextual detail views.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/resizable">Resizable</a></td><td>Simulation run list and detail panels.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/input">Input</a></td><td>Search and local form controls.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/field">Field</a></td><td>Labels and the note field in simulation run details.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/avatar">Avatar</a></td><td>Account identity in the workspace header.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a href="/docs/components/dicehub-logo">Dicehub Logo</a></td><td>Workspace mark in the global header.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
        </section>
        <section id="layouts" class="docs-section docs-section--component-examples">
          <h2><a class="docs-section-anchor" href="#layouts">Layouts</a></h2>
          {sidebarBlocks.map(block => <>
            <h3 id={block.id}><a class="docs-section-anchor" href={`#${block.id}`}>{block.title}</a></h3>
            <p>{block.description}</p>
            <div class="docs-component-example docs-block-example" data-block-example={block.id}>
              <div class="docs-block-example__bar"><span>Application Shell · {block.id}</span><a href={`/examples/appshell/${block.id}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open full example <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span><span class="docs-block-example__sr-only"> (new tab)</span></a></div>
              <div class="docs-component-preview docs-block-example__preview">{block.id === "hover-reveal" ? <SidebarHoverRevealDocsDemo client:load applicationShell /> : block.id === "minimal-workspace" ? <SidebarMinimalWorkspaceDocsDemo client:load /> : block.id === "paneled-workspace" ? <SidebarPaneledWorkspaceDocsDemo client:load /> : block.id === "workspace-pages" ? <SidebarWorkspacePagesDocsDemo client:load /> : block.id === "inbox-navigation" ? <SidebarInboxDocsDemo client:load /> : block.id === "dropdown-submenus" ? <SidebarDropdownSubmenusDocsDemo client:load /> : block.id === "submenus" || block.id === "floating-submenus" || block.id === "collapsible-submenus" ? <SidebarSubmenusDocsDemo client:load floating={block.id === "floating-submenus"} collapsible={block.id === "collapsible-submenus"} /> : block.id === "grouped" || block.id === "collapsible-sections" ? <SidebarGroupedDocsDemo client:load collapsibleSections={block.id === "collapsible-sections"} /> : <SidebarBlocksDocsDemo client:load variant={block.id === "inset-navigation" ? "inset" : block.id === "collapsible-icons" || block.id === "peeking" ? "workspace" : block.id} iconNavigation={block.id === "collapsible-icons"} insetNavigation={block.id === "inset-navigation"} peeking={block.id === "peeking"} />}</div>
              <p class="docs-block-example__starter-note">Copy this shell starter and add your application content. Layout styles and responsive panels come from Kappa. <a href={`/examples/appshell/starter/${block.id}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open this code</a></p>
              <CodeBlock code={exampleSource(block.id)} lang="vue" collapsible revealId={`sidebar-block-${block.id}-code`} />
            </div>
          </>)}
        </section>
        <section id="installation" class="docs-section">
          <h2><a class="docs-section-anchor" href="#installation">Installation and Usage</a></h2>
          <p>Import <code>AppShell</code> from <code>@dicehub/kappa/blocks/app-shell</code>. Load <code>@dicehub/kappa/styles/kappa.css</code> and <code>@dicehub/kappa/styles/theme-kappa.css</code> once in your application. Set your licensed font through <code>--kappa-font-sans</code>.</p>
          <p>Each code sample is a complete Vue component. It includes a few sample records and needs no custom CSS, local helper files, or asset downloads. The larger previews show how you can add search, menus, tables, and page actions.</p>
          <CodeBlock code={exampleSource("workspace")} lang="vue" collapsible revealId="sidebar-layout-usage-code" />
          <p>Use <code>fill</code> for a full-window application. Otherwise give the parent a height. The navigation toggle and mobile Close button are built in. Your application owns navigation, data, and persistence.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="content-alignment" class="docs-section">
          <h2><a class="docs-section-anchor" href="#content-alignment">Content Alignment</a></h2>
          <p>By default, content stays centered in the complete shell while there is enough room for the page and navigation. This follows the dicehub client: space opposite the sidebar balances its width, then shrinks before the page becomes narrower. Sidebar collapse and resize keep the page in place when it fits. Content can move and reflow when space is limited.</p>
          <CodeBlock code={`<AppShell.Root content-max-width="44rem" :mobile-breakpoint="1200" fill>
  <template #navigation><!-- Your navigation --></template>
  <AppShell.Page>
    Your page content.
  </AppShell.Page>
</AppShell.Root>`} lang="vue" />
          <p>Set <code>contentMaxWidth</code> to the page's maximum width, including its padding. The default is 72rem. Keep the same value in embedded and full-screen examples. With shell alignment, avoid adding a smaller maximum width inside this region: the shell needs the page width to balance it correctly.</p>
          <p>Responsive navigation uses the viewport width by default. Desktop gallery previews keep their sidebars visible, and selecting a page keeps navigation open. The reading examples use a 1200px viewport breakpoint. Set <code>mobile-breakpoint-reference="container"</code> explicitly for layouts that must follow their own width. Opening a mobile drawer or temporarily revealing collapsed navigation does not move the underlying content.</p>
          <p>Use <code>content-alignment="available"</code> for views that must fill all space beside navigation, such as a wide data grid or canvas. This mode ignores <code>contentMaxWidth</code>.</p>
          <p>Paneled Workspace uses available alignment: its main region fills the space beside navigation and moves as the navigation changes width. Its sidebar starts at 240px and uses the same Kappa resize handle as Minimal Workspace. Drag the edge or use the arrow keys to resize between 200px and 420px. The edge appears on hover, keyboard focus, and drag. The sidebar retains its width after collapsing to the 44px icon rail or visiting the mobile layout; the resize handle is hidden on mobile. The Your work heading and collapse control stay above the scrolling navigation; Dashboard is its first item. The workspace switcher stays in the sidebar footer. Help sits at the bottom of the right tools rail. Navigation text and page content are 14px. Top-level navigation links and submenu links use the same 2px row gap through Kappa Sidebar.Menu and Sidebar.MenuSub. At tablet sizes, the panel has 8px side spacing. Below 992px, the tools rail sits below the panel; below 576px, the panel frame reaches the screen edges. Paneled pages keep 12px inner side padding on phones and tablets. Minimal Workspace uses shell alignment, which keeps bounded content centered while the full shell has enough room.</p>
          <p>The aerospace project example mixes wide simulation run lists with centered reading pages. <a href="/examples/sidebar/paneled-workspace?view=Project+activity">Project activity</a>, <a href="/examples/sidebar/paneled-workspace?view=Overview">Overview</a>, and <a href="/examples/sidebar/paneled-workspace?view=Flight+test+plan">Flight test plan</a> have a 1006px maximum content width inside the full-width panel. The breadcrumb uses Kappa Breadcrumbs with <code>/</code> separators. Both the project and current page are real links, with <code>aria-current="page"</code> on the current link. Links underline on hover and show a focus outline on keyboard focus or press. Normal clicks navigate within the demo; modified clicks and the browser link menu retain native link behavior. In embedded previews, link URLs point to the full workspace example. The breadcrumb and tools keep their positions. On smaller screens, content uses the available width and keeps the page padding. The inner reading column uses the same balancing rule as Minimal Workspace: space opposite the sidebar keeps it centered in the complete shell. The content stays in place when navigation collapses or resizes if there is enough room. During a drag, both navigation and balancing space follow the pointer without a width animation. This balancing space shrinks before the page becomes narrower. The panel frame continues to fill the available width, and Simulation runs keeps its wide layout.</p>
          <p>Paneled Workspace starts with Dashboard and Simulation runs, followed by Projects, Templates, and Engineering tools. Projects starts with an Overview link to <a href="/examples/sidebar/paneled-workspace?view=All%20projects">all 24 projects</a>, then six project shortcuts. The full list uses Kappa Input, Tabs, Dropdown, and Table to search, filter by status, and sort by name. Search matches names, project codes, disciplines, descriptions, and leads. All projects are also available through Ctrl + K search and direct URLs.</p>
          <p>Select a project to replace the workspace links with its navigation: Overview, Project activity, Simulation, and Analytics. Simulation contains Simulation runs, Results, and Storage. Analytics contains Reports. Back to Projects sits above the Project heading in the fixed sidebar header and returns to the full project list. The back link has no permanent highlight; it uses the normal hover and pressed states, with 26px of space below it and no separator line. The collapsed rail uses the standard icon hover state. The example composes <code>Sidebar.SlidingViews</code> and <code>Sidebar.SlidingView</code> with a separate <code>Sidebar.Content</code> scroll region in each view. Both views stay mounted. Forward navigation slides left by one sidebar width; returning slides right. Direct URLs and reloads resolve the initial context without a selection animation. This example uses a 250ms transform transition with <code>cubic-bezier(0.77, 0, 0.175, 1)</code> and no fade. The header and footer stay fixed, inactive links are inert, and reduced motion removes the slide. The existing Sidebar parts manage focus when the active view changes. Entering or switching projects keeps the mobile drawer open; selecting a page closes it. Back to Projects and the switcher’s Go to all projects link restore focus to the project list’s Overview link.</p>
          <p>Inside a project, its name appears directly below the Project heading in the sidebar header, with a square project avatar and double chevrons to open the project switcher. This example combines Kappa ToolbarButton, Popover, and CommandPalette.Panel. The popup focuses search on opening, lists all 24 projects in name order, and filters by name or project code. The current project has a trailing checkmark; the highlighted row has its own background. Project options are links, so modified clicks and the browser link menu keep their native behavior. The list scrolls independently above the count and Go to all projects link. Selecting a project closes the popup and opens its Overview without leaving project navigation. Escape restores focus to the trigger. The popup uses the standard caret and a 100ms scale and fade; reduced motion removes the animation. Kappa Avatar shows the project image when avatarSrc is set, or the first letter of the project name as its fallback. The collapsed rail keeps the avatar with the full name in its tooltip. In the mobile drawer, the popup stays inside the drawer for keyboard focus and uses the available screen width.</p>
          <p>The standalone example stores the project code with the page in the URL, for example <code>?view=Simulation+runs&amp;project=HC-02</code>. Project context survives reload, browser Back, and opening links in new tabs. Previous project-name links still work. Selecting Dashboard, a workspace page, or another namespace leaves the project context. Breadcrumbs use the namespace and selected project, for example Ros.Space / Helicopter / Simulation runs. Dashboard keeps Ros.Space / Dashboard, and the logo returns there from either navigation view. Each project summary shows its description, status, lead, phase, and study goals. The legacy Project Settings page changes the selected project name throughout this preview. Project codes keep links stable after a rename; names reset on reload. When the tools rail moves below the content, Help stays at its right end. The namespace menu includes Create Group, which opens the local group creation preview, and Show all groups, which lists Engineering and Research. Selecting a group opens its workspace. Minimal Workspace uses New group for its creation action.</p>
          <p>Templates starts with an Overview link to All templates, followed by CFD Templates, Experimental, and My Published Templates. All templates combines the 18 sample templates; each category shows six. The pages use Kappa Grid, Card, Badge, and Button. Templates cover aerodynamics, thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, and multiphysics. Preview opens a Kappa Dialog with the analysis type, version, publisher, and included setup. Project activity has category filters and earlier updates; Flight test plan has status filters and selectable test details. These pages use local sample data, and earlier example URLs still resolve.</p>
          <p>Engineering tools contains Y+ calculator and Boundary layer height calculator. Both use Kappa Field and Select controls for velocity, distance from the leading edge, kinematic viscosity, and a laminar or turbulent flow model. Y+ estimates wall-to-cell-center distance and twice that distance as first-cell height. Boundary layer height estimates physical δ₉₉, not mesh layer count or growth ratio. Both show the local flat-plate correlations and assume incompressible flow, a smooth wall, and zero pressure gradient. Results update as inputs change; invalid or unrepresentable values hide the result. These pages are also available in collapsed navigation, the mobile drawer, and search.</p>
          <p>Minimal Workspace uses the same <code>AppShell.SearchTrigger</code> and <code>AppShell.SearchDialog</code> as Paneled Workspace. The trigger stays centered in the header; its dialog opens at the top of the window with the shared 160ms opening animation and 90ms closing fade. Reduced motion removes these animations. It keeps the shared search size, radius, and separate, borderless Ctrl and K hints; below 576px it shows only the search icon. Minimal Workspace and Paneled Workspace use a faint search border at rest and strengthen it on hover, press, keyboard focus, or while search is open. These local styles keep the control size unchanged between states. Search result labels in both examples use 14px text, with 12px secondary descriptions. Their 32px rows have 4px corners and a neutral hover fill that clears when the pointer leaves. Keyboard navigation and pressing a row show an inset focus ring. There is no left selection marker. Rows use shared dropdown color tokens, and reduced motion removes their transitions. Project search includes Overview and Project activity; workspace search offers project pages only after a project is selected. Search lists use the current navigation and project data; Paneled Workspace also includes simulation runs and namespace choices. Its search covers pages and runs together, without a filter menu. Its navigation uses the paneled example's 14px text, 21px line height, regular weight 425, selected weight 600, and shared navigation state colors. The namespace switcher uses double up/down chevrons. The collapse button uses the same double chevrons as Hover Reveal and sits to the right of the switcher. It appears on row hover or keyboard focus, with a reduced-motion-aware fade. In the collapsed rail, the expand button stays visible below the namespace icon; touch devices also keep the control visible. Help remains in the footer. Mobile navigation retains 44px touch targets and its own Close button.</p>
          <p>Migration: set <code>content-alignment="available"</code> to keep the previous alignment. SidebarLayout and Sidebar.Provider both retain the viewport breakpoint default.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="api" class="docs-section">
          <h2><a class="docs-section-anchor" href="#api">API and Composition</a></h2>
          <p><code>AppShell.Root</code> composes <code>SidebarLayout</code> and keeps its props, events, and slots. Existing SidebarLayout applications remain supported.</p>
          <div class="docs-api-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><code>Root</code></td><td>The header, navigation, mobile drawer, content area, and optional tools rail. Use <code>appearance="default | compact | paneled"</code>. The default appearance supports workspace, rail, inset, floating, split, and header variants.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Panels</code></td><td>A single inset frame with primary content, a detail pane, and an optional side panel. Each panel scrolls separately.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Page</code></td><td>Page padding, a title, description, actions slot, and 13px body text. Creates a main landmark; use one per page.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Toolbar / Actions</code></td><td>Panel headers and aligned action groups.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SearchTrigger / SearchDialog</code></td><td>A compact search button and a search dialog at the top of the window. Compose CommandPalette inside the dialog. The optional shortcut label is a hint; your application binds that key.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p>Root slots: <code>header</code>, <code>navigation</code>, <code>footer</code>, <code>toolbar-start</code>, <code>toolbar-trigger</code>, <code>toolbar</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>rail</code>, and the default content. Each receives the <a href="/docs/components/sidebar#events-and-context">Sidebar context</a>. The secondary slot is available for <code>variant="split"</code>.</p>
          <p>Put the brand link in <code>#toolbar-start</code> so it comes before the navigation toggle in display and keyboard order. The paneled mobile toggle uses a menu icon. Use <code>#toolbar-trigger</code> to replace the built-in toggle with your own <code>Sidebar.Trigger</code>; <code>triggerProps</code> configures the default toggle. The mobile header has an 8px gap between the brand and toggle, with an 8px left inset below 992px and 12px above it.</p>
          <p>The paneled mobile drawer is 80vw wide, up to 320px, with 16px outer corners. It slides in and out over 200ms with <code>ease</code> timing. The overlay uses <code>--kappa-shell-nav-backdrop</code>. Escape, an outside press, or the Close button closes it and returns focus to its opener. Reduced motion removes the visible slide, including when the preference changes during closing.</p>
          <p>Place tools in the Root <code>#rail</code> slot. The rail stays at the shell edge even when <code>AppShell.Panels</code> is absent. You can add or remove the panels with a conditional default slot. Below 992px the rail moves to the bottom edge. Set <code>rail-label</code> to translate its default accessible name, “Workspace tools”.</p>
          <p>Use <code>v-model:open</code>, <code>v-model:mobile-open</code>, and <code>v-model:resize-width</code> to control navigation. Keep page selection and section state outside the slots so mobile transitions preserve them.</p>
          <p>For the paneled project row, place <a href="/docs/components/avatar">Avatar</a> in the <code>Sidebar.MenuButton</code> icon slot. Use <code>kappa-app-shell__project-link</code> on the button and <code>kappa-app-shell__project-avatar</code> on Avatar.Root for a bold name and a 24px avatar with 4px corners. Pass the project's image URL to Avatar.Image and its first letter to Avatar.Fallback. Use an empty image alt because the adjacent project name labels the control. The starter keeps this row in the scrolling navigation so its avatar also remains visible when navigation collapses.</p>
          <p>The project row opens the project overview directly. Use <code>href</code> for a routed overview, or a click handler for local views, and bind <code>active</code> to the selected page. This row has no dropdown. The full demo places the shared WorkspaceSwitcher in the fixed sidebar footer and an icon-only Help ToolbarButton at the bottom of the right tools rail. Help uses the same size, color, and interaction states as the other rail controls. Its menu uses the shared Dropdown caret, border, and shadow. A Version 0.1.2 header precedes three groups: Help, Support, Documentation, and Download the CLI; Terms of service and Privacy policy; Keyboard shortcuts and What’s new. Rows use leading icons. Help, Support, Documentation, and the policy links open local preview pages in new tabs. The ? key opens Keyboard shortcuts outside text fields and popups; Download the CLI and Log out are preview actions. The Help menu opens to the left of the right rail, or above the bottom toolbar on smaller screens. Its tooltip follows the same direction. The workspace switcher shows its double up/down arrows on hover, keyboard focus, and while open, with a 200ms fade. Its menu opens upward with the shared Dropdown caret pointing to the namespace switcher; in collapsed navigation, the namespace menu opens beside its icon. It starts with Ros.Space selected and switches between Ros.Space, Engineering, and Research, updates the project and simulation runs, and keeps the current workspace selected in the menu. A separate double-chevron Sidebar.Trigger beside the Your work heading collapses navigation. This control appears when the header row is hovered or contains keyboard focus, with a 200ms opacity transition; it remains visible on devices without hover and in the collapsed rail. The same trigger retains focus across collapse and expansion. Reduced motion removes its transition. On mobile, the namespace menu stays inside the drawer and selecting a workspace closes the drawer. Help remains available in the bottom toolbar when the drawer is closed. Workspace selection is also available in search.</p>
          <p>Paneled Workspace and Hover Reveal use the same astronaut image as Minimal Workspace for the Ros.Space personal workspace. Their custom triggers place a 24px Kappa Avatar in the icon slot, with initials if the image cannot load. The shared WorkspaceSwitcher uses the same image in its menu. Ros.Space has a circular avatar in the trigger, menu header, and menu option. Engineering and Research retain their workspace icons.</p>
          <p>Inset Navigation uses the Paneled Workspace navigation button style: 14px text with a 21px line height, 28px desktop rows, 2px row gaps, 8px corners, and 16px icons in 24px slots. Hover, press, keyboard focus, and selection use the shared shell navigation tokens. The selected row has a left indicator and 600 font weight; a group shows selection when its selected child is hidden. Nested links keep the same row spacing and align below the parent label. Mobile rows retain a 44px minimum touch target. These styles apply only to this example and follow its navigation into the mobile drawer.</p>
          <p>Hover Reveal keeps one workspace switcher in the fixed sidebar footer. Its navigation rows and context tabs use the same 14px text, 28px desktop height, 8px corners, and hover, pressed, and selected colors as Paneled Workspace. Page rows have 2px gaps and a left selection indicator; a closed group shows the indicator when it contains the selected page. Mobile controls retain 44px touch targets. The workspace menu uses a 1px separator with the subtle line token. The Find a workspace page action uses the Button icon prop for the standard small-button search icon. Its menu opens upward, while the header contains only the always-visible desktop collapse button or the mobile Close button. The same footer stays available in the temporary hover panel. Drag the left navigation edge to resize, or double-click it to restore the default width; keyboard steps and the quiet drag indicator match the side-panel controls.</p>
          <p>For a corner home link, add <code>kappa-app-shell__brand</code> to a labelled link containing <code>DicehubLogo variant="glyph" aria-hidden="true"</code>. In Paneled Workspace, the logo opens Dashboard; its link URL also targets Dashboard for new-tab and modified clicks. The glyph is 24px high. Each dice face changes colour on hover in 250ms and returns to its theme ink in 800ms. Continuous hover areas cover letter cutouts and the gaps between faces, so pointer movement does not interrupt the highlight. Keyboard focus highlights all three faces and shows a focus outline. Reduced motion removes the transitions.</p>
          <p>Selected paneled rows show a 2px indicator at the sidebar edge, with a 6px gap before the row and 4px vertical insets. A separator divides the starter's project row from its navigation groups. The full demo omits the footer separator and uses the navigation scroll shadow when more items remain below. The full demo uses category navigation and the search palette to open pages.</p>
          <p>When paneled navigation has more items below, a 36px bottom fade marks the scroll edge above the footer. It clears as the last item comes into view and stays hidden when the list fits. The native scrolling background follows resizing and expanded groups without extra spacing or animation. Its color uses <code>--kappa-shell-nav-scroll-shadow</code> in both themes.</p>
          <p>Long navigation labels use a single line and an ellipsis in expanded navigation, mobile navigation, and collapsed flyouts. A text span inside each flex row lets the label shrink without clipping counts or chevrons. Submenu rows have 12px of padding at the end so the ellipsis stays clear of the button edge. The full page name remains available to assistive technology and through the native hover title.</p>
          <p>Paneled navigation and its flyouts use 14px text: weight 425 normally and 600 for selection. The <code>--kappa-shell-nav-*</code> tokens define text, hover, focus, selection, and pressed colors in both themes. Pressed rows have a stronger background; keyboard focus keeps a visible outline.</p>
          <p>Paneled categories expand and collapse their measured content height over 200ms with <code>ease</code> timing. The row gap is part of that height, so the groups below move continuously. The demo labels enter over 150ms and chevrons turn over 200ms with <code>cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1)</code>. Reduced motion removes the visible movement on desktop and mobile. Closing still completes if that preference changes during a collapse.</p>
          <p>Closed desktop categories show a compact flyout. In the icon-only sidebar, a bold section heading and divider identify the menu; the expanded sidebar omits this repeated heading. The flyout has 4px top and side padding, 2px bottom padding, 28px rows with a 2px gap, and 8px outer corners. It fades in over 200ms. A transparent 12px pointer margin surrounds the surface and bridges the 10px gap. A temporary pointer corridor protects diagonal movement to lower items for up to one second, so nearby category buttons do not replace the menu. The corridor allows 5px of sideways drift on the menu-facing half of the trigger. It stays active across the outer margin and section heading, and is removed when the pointer reaches the menu list. Straight movement through the icon centers still switches categories. HoverCard uses a 5ms handoff when the pointer leaves, allowing entry into the flyout while clearing its highlight promptly when the pointer moves elsewhere. Focused links keep the popup open; Escape and navigation close it immediately. Its surface and close shadow use <code>--kappa-shell-nav-flyout-*</code> tokens. Selected popup links use bold text and the selected background; the edge indicator stays on the sidebar category.</p>
          <p>Navigation counts use a 24px end slot with 8px inner end spacing in both inline rows and flyouts. This keeps counts away from the row edge while the page label uses the remaining width.</p>
          <p>When a category closes around the current page, set <code>data-active-descendant</code> on its trigger to show the selected style. Keep <code>aria-current="page"</code> on the actual page link. The full demo opens closed desktop categories on hover or focus; Arrow Down enters the flyout and Escape returns focus to the trigger. The current page stays selected in the flyout. Set <code>data-flyout-open</code> on the trigger to keep its hover style while the pointer is in that flyout. Clicking the category expands its inline links; mobile navigation uses inline links only.</p>
          <p>Panels slots: default content, <code>header</code>, <code>detail</code>, and <code>aside</code>. Control visibility with <code>v-model:detail-open</code> and <code>v-model:aside-open</code>. The detail slot receives <code>close</code> and <code>mode</code>; the aside slot receives <code>close</code>, <code>toggleMaximized</code>, <code>maximized</code>, and <code>canMaximize</code>.</p>
          <p>Details overlay the page below <code>splitAt</code> (1200px of available panel width). Above it, an accessible splitter separates the panels. Use <code>v-model:size</code> for persistent percentage sizes and <code>minSize</code> (30 by default) for the minimum panel share. The side panel overlays below 992px of shell width, docks above it, and can maximize. Its outer edge stays fixed during maximize and restore, so controls aligned to that edge do not move. When maximized to the full frame, the side panel has no inner divider. On very wide screens, maximization keeps part of the primary panel visible and retains the divider.</p>
          <p>Drag the docked side panel's 4px inner edge to change its width. The panel follows the pointer without a width transition. Its width stays between 400px and the smaller of 960px, 60% of the shell width, or the space left after reserving 320px for the main panel. If space is tighter, the 400px side-panel minimum takes priority. The chosen width survives closing, maximizing, and switching to mobile; narrower layouts clamp the visible width without discarding that choice. The handle is hidden in mobile overlays and maximized panels.</p>
          <p>Focus the separator and use Left or Right to move it by 24px; Shift makes larger steps. Home and End move it to its limits, following Ark Splitter behavior. Double-click restores the responsive default. Use <code>v-model:aside-width</code> for a controlled pixel width; reset emits <code>undefined</code>. Set <code>aside-resize-label</code> to name the separator; the default is “Resize side panel”. Keyboard focus remains visible, and reduced motion removes maximize and restore animations.</p>
          <p>Set <code>detailLabel</code>, <code>asideLabel</code>, and <code>resizeLabel</code> for accessible names. Optional <code>initialDetailFocus</code>, <code>finalDetailFocus</code>, <code>initialAsideFocus</code>, and <code>finalAsideFocus</code> callbacks select focus targets. Closing a panel returns focus to its opener by default.</p>
          <p>For the paneled appearance, <code>bloom</code> adds the blue and teal background. The artwork is included in the package. Panel shadows use <code>--kappa-panel-shadow</code>. Keep overflow visible around the panel frame so its outer shadow remains visible; scrolling belongs inside each panel.</p>
          <p>The examples use <KbdGroup><Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd><span>+</span><Kbd>K</Kbd></KbdGroup> to open search and leave ordinary slash input unchanged. Set <code>shortcut="Ctrl + K"</code> on SearchTrigger to display separate Kbd elements with a plus between them. In shell search, these use plain text without borders, backgrounds, shadows, or keycap padding. This prop displays the hint; the application binds the shortcut. SearchTrigger uses 10px corners, 14px text at weight 320, and subtle 12px shortcut hints at weight 400. Its surface and borders use the <code>--kappa-shell-search-*</code> theme tokens: the shared border strengthens on hover while the surface stays stable in both themes.</p>
          <p>The paneled demo uses <a href="/docs/components/button#toolbar-buttons">ToolbarButton</a> for its toolbar counters and AI chat and Activities rail controls. Counters use the default ghost appearance and 32px size. The component owns the 16px icons, 8px corners, balanced padding, hover and pressed colors, and keyboard focus ring through <code>--kappa-toolbar-button-*</code> tokens. Counts are included in the accessible names. Pressing does not move the buttons; reduced motion removes transitions. The demo supplies action handlers and group layout.</p>
          <p>The Runs shortcut shows the number of simulations with a Running status in the selected namespace. Queued, completed, failed, and cancelled runs do not contribute to this count. It opens the same simulation list used by navigation and search, built with Kappa Input, Tabs, Table, and Badge. Status tabs filter All runs, Running, Queued, Completed, Failed, and Cancelled. Selecting a run opens its details with compute allocation, runtime, cost, and local notes. New simulation run adds a queued example, and Cancel simulation run changes only the local status. No solver or scheduler is connected. The Dashboard, active-run count, credit usage, and AI summary use this same run data. Earlier work-item URLs redirect to Simulation runs; retired task-tracking pages are excluded from search. The adjacent Credits control uses a transparent ToolbarButton with no visible border and the standard Popover caret. Its decorative 14px MatrixLoader replaces the balance only while the initial credits value loads. The example resolves its local credits data on mount; after that, the balance replaces the loader. Run activity does not control the loader. The compact popup shows available credits, a usage badge, and a Progress bar for a 30-day balance target at the current hourly rate. With no active usage, a positive balance fills the bar and the copy reads “Your balance is ready when you are.” Buy credits and Redeem credits open small local forms; they add demo credits only, with no payment or billing service. DICEHUB-DEMO can be redeemed once per namespace in each preview session. Go to billing opens the balance and per-run cost table. The balance, run counts, and costs use synthetic data, remain separate for each namespace and preview instance, and reset on reload. The toolbar order is Credits, Runs, Notifications, then the profile avatar. It opens a shared Dropdown with notification titles, descriptions, times, unread markers, and Mark all as read. Selecting a notification marks it read and opens its related page. Read state is separate for each namespace. On narrow screens, the notification icon remains available while its count and the Credits text label are hidden. The credits chevron stays fixed; hover, press, keyboard focus, and open states keep the shared toolbar styling.</p>
          <p>AI chat and Activities use separate 24px ghost ToolbarButtons in <code>#rail</code>. They switch the shared side pane: Activities opens the activity feed beside the page on desktop, while AI chat opens a conversation with the shared MessageComposer. The chat uses clearly labeled local sample replies; no AI service is connected. Conversation history and drafts remain separate per namespace and survive panel changes. The shell gives ghost rail controls a transparent default surface with no border, a neutral hover and keyboard-focus fill, and a darker pointer-down fill. <code>aria-expanded="true"</code> keeps the highlight and adds an edge marker until the panel closes. The marker sits on the right on desktop and below the button in the mobile bottom rail. These states use <code>--kappa-shell-rail-highlight</code> and <code>--kappa-shell-rail-pressed</code>, with the Kappa focus color for the marker and inset keyboard-focus ring. State colors change immediately. Rail tooltips keep the shared 500ms delay, appear on the left on desktop and above the bottom rail on small screens, and stay hidden while their own panel is open.</p>
          <p>The paneled demo's account control uses a 32px circular avatar with a subtle inner edge. Hover and press change the trigger background over 200ms; pressing does not move the avatar. The menu is 224px wide, starts 8px below the control, and has an 8px pointer, compact shadow, 14px text, and 32px rows. The name and username form one profile action. Account actions use icons and full-width dividers. Theme opens a shared Dropdown submenu with Light mode, Dark mode, and System radio options and a trailing checkmark. The selection is saved in browser storage; System follows device theme changes. Both standalone routes restore the theme before the page renders. Log out is the last item, below its own divider. In this example, it opens a local preview and leaves the session signed in. Ark Dropdown handles click, keyboard navigation, Escape, outside dismissal, and focus return; selecting a local page closes the menu. The avatar is a profile link: a plain click opens the menu, while modified clicks and middle clicks keep native link behavior. Menu links open the local view, or the standalone demo when opened in another tab. Reduced motion removes transitions. Shell account colors use <code>--kappa-shell-action-*</code>, <code>--kappa-shell-avatar-border</code>, and <code>--kappa-shell-menu-*</code> tokens in both themes.</p>
          <p>Account menu rows use a plain hover fill. The inset focus ring follows keyboard navigation and does not follow pointer hover. Pressed rows retain their inset ring. The avatar control shows a 1px inner ring and a 2px outer focus-color ring while pressed or keyboard-focused. This ring stays outside the photo so an opaque image cannot hide the pressed state. Releasing the pointer removes the press ring; opening the menu does not add a persistent selected style. Hover and press also change the background behind the photo without moving the avatar. The explicit press ring keeps this feedback independent of Ark's pointer focus handling.</p>
          <div class="docs-component-example"><div class="docs-component-preview docs-block-example__preview"><SidebarLayoutStateDocsDemo client:load /></div><CodeBlock code={controlledSource} lang="vue" collapsible revealId="sidebar-layout-controlled-code" /></div>
        </section>
        <section id="accessibility" class="docs-section">
          <h2><a class="docs-section-anchor" href="#accessibility">Accessibility and Styling</a></h2>
          <p>Sidebar, Dialog, and Resizable provide keyboard navigation, focus handling, and accessible resize controls. Detail and side panels are nonmodal: you can continue to use the page while they are open. Closed panels cannot receive focus. Search uses a modal CommandPalette.</p>
          <p>Use a single main landmark. For a detail pane, pass <code>as="section"</code> to Page. Give icon buttons accessible names. Connect navigation to your router, and close the mobile drawer after navigation succeeds.</p>
          <p>The shell uses Kappa colors and your font token in light and dark modes. Motion respects reduced-motion preferences. Layout CSS, bloom assets, and panel sizing are internal to the block.</p>
          <p>Page content uses 14px text (<code>0.875rem</code>) with a 1.5 line height. The shell examples use the same body size. Headings, badges, and secondary metadata keep their own type sizes.</p>
        </section>
